  <div id="sutta">
  
                <h3 id="AN5.165" class="h1">On Asking Questions<br/>
                  Pañhapucchā Sutta  (AN 5:165)</h3><!--end suttahead-->
                <p>Then Ven. Sāriputta addressed the monks: “Friend monks.”</p>
                <p>“Yes, friend,” the monks responded to him.</p>
                <p>Ven. Sāriputta said: “All those who ask questions of another do so from any one of five motivations. Which five?</p>
                <p>“One asks a question of another through stupidity &amp; bewilderment. One asks a question of another through evil desires &amp; overwhelmed with greed. One asks a question of another through contempt. One asks a question of another when desiring knowledge. Or one asks a question with this thought,<span class="fn"><a href="#an5.165note01">1</a></span><!--end fn cite--> ‘If, when asked, he answers correctly, well &amp; good. If not, then I will answer correctly (for him).’</p>
                <p>“All those who ask questions of another do so from any one of these five motivations. And as for me, when I ask a question of another, it’s with this thought: ‘If, when asked, he answers correctly, well &amp; good. If not, then I will answer correctly (for him).’</p>
                <div class="note">
                  <p class="notetitle">Note</p><!--end notetitle-->
                  <p id="an5.165note01">1. Reading <em>panevaṁ-citto</em> with the Thai edition. The PTS reading—<em>pakuppanto citto,</em> “with a provoked heart”—does not fit the context at all.</p>
                </div><!--end notes-->
                <p class="seealso">See also: <a href="AN3_68.html">AN 3:68</a>; <a href="AN4_42.html">AN 4:42</a></p><!--end seealso-->
      </div><!--end:sutta-->
    </div><!--end:content-->
    
